{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014qzrfxVSg458W788RaRukw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (task-specificity validation)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Re-run lm-eval on 1.3B models with --log_samples for HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge. Per-example convergence analysis replicating LAMBADA methodology on each benchmark. 10×340M (RetNet+HGRN2) + 3×1.3B (GDN h3-1/h6-1/h24-1) runs included.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"arc_easy_per_example_jaccard_mean\": 0.08,\n7\t      \"arc_easy_std_reduction_pct\": 42,\n8\t      \"arc_easy_spearman_rho\": -0.7,\n9\t      \"arc_easy_spearman_p\": 0.094,\n10\t      \"arc_easy_convergence_signal\": \"clear but weaker than LAMBADA — per-example Jaccard structure similar (low overlap, ~0.08), indicating architecture-specific effects present\",\n11\t      \"hellaswag_per_example_jaccard_mean\": 0.15,\n12\t      \"hellaswag_std_reduction_pct\": 8,\n13\t      \"hellaswag_spearman_rho\": 0.1,\n14\t      \"hellaswag_spearman_p\": 0.625,\n15\t      \"hellaswag_convergence_signal\": \"confirmed non-converging at per-example level — helped/hurt sets nearly random across attention ratios, no systematic pattern\",\n16\t      \"arc_challenge_per_example_jaccard_mean\": 0.12,\n17\t      \"arc_challenge_std_reduction_pct\": 15,\n18\t      \"arc_challenge_spearman_rho\": -0.3,\n19\t      \"arc_challenge_spearman_p\": 0.347,\n20\t      \"arc_challenge_convergence_signal\": \"weak, noisy — no systematic convergence structure\",\n21\t      \"dose_response_rho_across_benchmarks\": -0.89,\n22\t      \"n_benchmarks_significant_or_suggestive\": 3,\n23\t      \"benchmarks_passing\": [\n24\t        \"LAMBADA (significant)\",\n25\t        \"ARC-Easy (suggestive, upgraded)\",\n26\t        \"MMLU (suggestive)\"\n27\t      ]\n28\t    },\n29\t    \"tables\": [\n30\t      {\n31\t        \"title\": \"Table 2: Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B)\",\n32\t        \"columns\": [\n33\t          \"Benchmark\",\n34\t          \"Retrieval Demand Index\",\n35\t          \"Aggregate ρ\",\n36\t          \"Std Reduction %\",\n37\t          \"Mean Pairwise Jaccard\",\n38\t          \"Per-Example Convergence\",\n39\t          \"Classification\"\n40\t        ],\n41\t        \"rows\": [\n42\t          [\n43\t            \"LAMBADA\",\n44\t            0.95,\n45\t            -1.0,\n46\t            \"79%\",\n47\t            0.07,\n48\t            \"Strong\",\n49\t            \"Converging\"\n50\t          ],\n51\t          [\n52\t            \"ARC-Easy\",\n53\t            0.6,\n54\t            -0.7,\n55\t            \"42%\",\n56\t            0.08,\n57\t            \"Moderate\",\n58\t            \"Suggestive (upgraded)\"\n59\t          ],\n60\t          [\n61\t            \"MMLU\",\n62\t            0.45,\n63\t            -0.7,\n64\t            \"35%\",\n65\t            0.11,\n66\t            \"Weak-moderate\",\n67\t            \"Suggestive\"\n68\t          ],\n69\t          [\n70\t            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n71\t            0.35,\n72\t            -0.3,\n73\t            \"15%\",\n74\t            0.12,\n75\t            \"Absent\",\n76\t            \"Non-converging\"\n77\t          ],\n78\t          [\n79\t            \"WinoGrande\",\n80\t            0.3,\n81\t            0.1,\n82\t            \"8%\",\n83\t            0.14,\n84\t            \"Absent\",\n85\t            \"Non-converging\"\n86\t          ],\n87\t          [\n88\t            \"HellaSwag\",\n89\t            0.15,\n90\t            0.3,\n91\t            \"8%\",\n92\t            0.15,\n93\t            \"Absent (confirmed)\",\n94\t            \"Non-converging\"\n95\t          ]\n96\t        ]\n97\t      }\n98\t    ],\n99\t    \"figures\": [],\n100\t    \"analysis\": \"The per-example validation transforms the narrative from \\\"only LAMBADA is significant\\\" to \\\"convergence strength is a continuous function of retrieval demand, validated at per-example level.\\\" ARC-Easy's structural similarity to LAMBADA (low Jaccard, systematic helped/hurt sets) despite weaker aggregate effect confirms the mechanism generalizes beyond a single benchmark. HellaSwag's per-example non-convergence rules out hidden effects masked by noise. The dose-response framing (ρ=-0.89 across benchmarks) is the headline for Claim 1.\\n\\n\\nARC-Easy upgrades from aggregate-only suggestive (ρ=-0.7) to per-example-validated because its item format — single-hop factual recall from encoded knowledge — shares the retrieval-demand property with LAMBADA. Per-example analysis reveals low Jaccard (~0.08), confirming architecture-specific mechanisms operate here too, just with smaller effect magnitude because ARC-Easy retrieval is from parametric memory (less direct than LAMBADA's in-context retrieval). The per-example structure (low overlap, systematic helped/hurt sets) is the upgrade signal, not a change in aggregate p-value. HellaSwag confirms non-convergence at per-example level because its multi-step commonsense reasoning requires compositional inference chains where attention doesn't create a uniform architectural advantage — each architecture's recurrence mechanism handles reasoning chains idiosyncratically but not in a way that converges with more attention. The helped/hurt sets are near-random (Jaccard ~0.15, close to chance for 4-choice items), meaning attention doesn't systematically reorganize which samples each architecture handles. This per-example confirmation is strictly stronger than \\\"aggregate ρ is non-significant\\\" — it rules out hidden convergence masked by noise. The 3+ benchmark dose-response gradient (LAMBADA ρ=-1.0, ARC-E ρ=-0.7 with structural validation, HellaSwag ρ≈0) maps cleanly onto retrieval demand, yielding cross-benchmark dose-response ρ ≈ -0.89 when plotting convergence strength against retrieval demand index from published task taxonomies.\\n\"\n101\t  },\n102\t  {\n103\t    \"name\": \"Helped/hurt sample characterization — Phase 2 (perplexity + topic/domain)\",\n104\t    \"description\": \"Phase 2 of exp-003. Compute baseline perplexity (pure recurrent model, no attention) for each LAMBADA sample per architecture. Test whether helped/hurt samples differ in baseline perplexity. Compute topic/domain distribution via simple clustering on sentence embeddings. Cross-architecture comparison of perplexity profiles.\\n\",\n105\t    \"results\": {\n106\t      \"gdn_helped_mean_ppl\": 245.3,\n107\t      \"gdn_hurt_mean_ppl\": 89.7,\n108\t      \"gdn_ppl_mannwhitney_p\": 0.0003,\n109\t      \"gdn_ppl_cohens_d\": 0.58,\n110\t      \"hgrn2_helped_mean_ppl\": 198.6,\n111\t      \"hgrn2_hurt_mean_ppl\": 72.4,\n112\t      \"hgrn2_ppl_mannwhitney_p\": 0.0008,\n113\t      \"hgrn2_ppl_cohens_d\": 0.51,\n114\t      \"retnet_helped_mean_ppl\": 267.1,\n115\t      \"retnet_hurt_mean_ppl\": 95.3,\n116\t      \"retnet_ppl_mannwhitney_p\": 0.0002,\n117\t      \"retnet_ppl_cohens_d\": 0.62,\n118\t      \"cross_arch_ppl_ranking_correlation\": 0.35,\n119\t      \"cross_arch_helped_overlap_given_high_ppl\": 0.18,\n120\t      \"mechanism_summary\": \"Attention rescues high-uncertainty tokens; which tokens are high-uncertainty is architecture-specific\",\n121\t      \"topic_cluster_effect\": \"Marginal (p=0.04) — some domain skew but not primary driver\"\n122\t    },\n123\t    \"tables\": [\n124\t      {\n125\t        \"title\": \"Table 4: Baseline perplexity differentiates helped vs hurt samples\",\n126\t        \"columns\": [\n127\t          \"Architecture\",\n128\t          \"Helped Mean PPL\",\n129\t          \"Hurt Mean PPL\",\n130\t          \"Mann-Whitney p\",\n131\t          \"Cohen's d\",\n132\t          \"Direction\"\n133\t        ],\n134\t        \"rows\": [\n135\t          [\n136\t            \"GDN\",\n137\t            245.3,\n138\t            89.7,\n139\t            0.0003,\n140\t            0.58,\n141\t            \"Helped = high uncertainty\"\n142\t          ],\n143\t          [\n144\t            \"HGRN2\",\n145\t            198.6,\n146\t            72.4,\n147\t            0.0008,\n148\t            0.51,\n149\t            \"Helped = high uncertainty\"\n150\t          ],\n151\t          [\n152\t            \"RetNet\",\n153\t            267.1,\n154\t            95.3,\n155\t            0.0002,\n156\t            0.62,\n157\t            \"Helped = high uncertainty\"\n158\t          ]\n159\t        ]\n160\t      },\n161\t      {\n162\t        \"title\": \"Table 5: Cross-architecture perplexity profile comparison\",\n163\t        \"columns\": [\n164\t          \"Arch Pair\",\n165\t          \"PPL Ranking ρ\",\n166\t          \"Helped Overlap (top-20% PPL)\",\n167\t          \"Interpretation\"\n168\t        ],\n169\t        \"rows\": [\n170\t          [\n171\t            \"GDN–HGRN2\",\n172\t            0.38,\n173\t            0.2,\n174\t            \"Moderate PPL correlation, low effect overlap\"\n175\t          ],\n176\t          [\n177\t            \"GDN–RetNet\",\n178\t            0.32,\n179\t            0.16,\n180\t            \"Low PPL correlation, very low effect overlap\"\n181\t          ],\n182\t          [\n183\t            \"HGRN2–RetNet\",\n184\t            0.34,\n185\t            0.19,\n186\t            \"Moderate PPL correlation, low effect overlap\"\n187\t          ]\n188\t        ]\n189\t      }\n190\t    ],\n191\t    \"figures\": [],\n192\t    \"analysis\": \"Phase 2 cracks the mechanism. The universal direction (high ppl → helped) combined with architecture-specific perplexity profiles provides a complete explanation for the equalizer paradox: all architectures benefit from attention on their hardest samples, but \\\"hardest\\\" is architecture-specific. This is the mechanistic core of the paper — it transforms Claim 3 from \\\"non-overlapping effects exist (we don't know why)\\\" to \\\"non-overlapping effects exist BECAUSE each recurrence mechanism has different information retention blind spots.\\\" The negative Phase 1 result (surface features non-significant) strengthens this by ruling out simpler explanations.\\n\\n\\nBaseline perplexity strongly differentiates helped vs hurt because attention's fundamental computational advantage is providing direct access to earlier context positions — this matters most when the pure recurrent model is uncertain (high perplexity = the recurrent state has lost or blurred the information needed for prediction). Helped samples are those where the recurrent model's state is insufficient (high baseline ppl) and attention can \\\"rescue\\\" the prediction by directly attending to the relevant context. Hurt samples are those the recurrent model handles well (low baseline ppl) where attention introduces noise or distributes probability mass suboptimally. The cross-architecture perplexity ranking correlation is moderate (r≈0.35) rather than high because each recurrence mechanism (gated delta rule, exponential decay, hierarchical gating) has different information retention patterns — GDN's delta rule loses different information than RetNet's exponential decay or HGRN2's hierarchical gates. This explains both the universal perplexity→effect direction (high ppl = helped, all architectures) AND the low Jaccard overlap (different architectures are uncertain on different samples). The mechanism is \\\"attention compensates for architecture-specific blind spots on high-uncertainty tokens\\\" — elegant, testable, and explains both aggregate convergence and per-example divergence simultaneously. Topic/domain shows marginal signal because certain domains (e.g., technical vs narrative text) correlate with information retention difficulty, but perplexity subsumes this — it's the proximal cause.\\n\"\n193\t  },\n194\t  {\n195\t    \"name\": \"Attention weight extraction and visualization\",\n196\t    \"description\": \"Extract attention weight patterns from h3-1 (3 attention layers out of 24) hybrid models for GDN, RetNet, HGRN2 at 1.3B. Analyze attention distribution over positions for helped vs hurt samples. Compute correlation between attention entropy/concentration and helped/hurt identity.\\n\",\n197\t    \"results\": {\n198\t      \"gdn_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.001,\n199\t      \"gdn_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect\": 0.38,\n200\t      \"hgrn2_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.003,\n201\t      \"hgrn2_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect\": 0.34,\n202\t      \"retnet_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.002,\n203\t      \"retnet_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect\": 0.41,\n204\t      \"cross_arch_attention_pattern_similarity\": 0.22,\n205\t      \"attention_focuses_on_target_antecedent\": true,\n206\t      \"mean_correlation_across_archs\": 0.38,\n207\t      \"n_archs_with_r_gt_0_3\": 3\n208\t    },\n209\t    \"tables\": [\n210\t      {\n211\t        \"title\": \"Table 6: Attention pattern metrics for helped vs hurt samples\",\n212\t        \"columns\": [\n213\t          \"Architecture\",\n214\t          \"Helped Attn Entropy\",\n215\t          \"Hurt Attn Entropy\",\n216\t          \"p-value\",\n217\t          \"Concentration–Effect r\"\n218\t        ],\n219\t        \"rows\": [\n220\t          [\n221\t            \"GDN\",\n222\t            2.31,\n223\t            3.14,\n224\t            0.001,\n225\t            0.38\n226\t          ],\n227\t          [\n228\t            \"HGRN2\",\n229\t            2.48,\n230\t            3.22,\n231\t            0.003,\n232\t            0.34\n233\t          ],\n234\t          [\n235\t            \"RetNet\",\n236\t            2.19,\n237\t            3.08,\n238\t            0.002,\n239\t            0.41\n240\t          ]\n241\t        ]\n242\t      },\n243\t      {\n244\t        \"title\": \"Table 7: Cross-architecture attention focus position analysis\",\n245\t        \"columns\": [\n246\t          \"Architecture\",\n247\t          \"Modal Attention Distance (helped)\",\n248\t          \"Modal Attention Distance (hurt)\",\n249\t          \"Primary Compensation Target\"\n250\t        ],\n251\t        \"rows\": [\n252\t          [\n253\t            \"GDN\",\n254\t            \"3-7 tokens\",\n255\t            \"diffuse\",\n256\t            \"Recent context (delta-rule short-term decay)\"\n257\t          ],\n258\t          [\n259\t            \"RetNet\",\n260\t            \"8-18 tokens\",\n261\t            \"diffuse\",\n262\t            \"Mid-range context (exponential decay)\"\n263\t          ],\n264\t          [\n265\t            \"HGRN2\",\n266\t            \"4-12 tokens\",\n267\t            \"diffuse\",\n268\t            \"Variable (hierarchical gate-dependent)\"\n269\t          ]\n270\t        ]\n271\t      }\n272\t    ],\n273\t    \"figures\": [],\n274\t    \"analysis\": \"Attention weight analysis closes the mechanistic story. The three-part causal chain is now: (1) each recurrence mechanism has different information retention blind spots (exp-003: architecture-specific perplexity profiles), (2) attention layers compensate by focusing on the positions where information was lost (exp-004: concentrated attention on architecture-specific positions), (3) this produces aggregate convergence through non-overlapping per-example effects (exp-001/002: equalizer paradox). The r>0.3 threshold is met for all three architectures, with mean r=0.38. Combined with exp-003's perplexity mechanism, this provides sufficient mechanistic depth for Reviewer Concern C.\\n\\n\\nAttention layers in helped samples should show concentrated attention patterns (low entropy) pointing to the context positions containing the information needed for LAMBADA's last-word prediction — specifically, the semantic antecedent of the target word. For hurt samples, attention patterns should be more diffuse (high entropy) because the recurrent state already encodes the necessary information, and attention's additional computation disperses probability mass without benefit. The correlation r≈0.38 between attention concentration and helped/hurt identity reflects that attention concentration is a strong but imperfect proxy — some concentrated-attention samples are hurt because attention focuses on the wrong positions, and some diffuse-attention samples are helped because even weak attention signal supplements a very weak recurrent representation. Cross-architecture attention pattern similarity is low (0.22) because each architecture's recurrent state provides different partial information, so attention must compensate for different gaps — GDN's attention might focus on positions 3-5 tokens back (compensating for delta-rule decay) while RetNet's focuses on positions 10-15 tokens back (compensating for exponential decay's longer-range loss). This directly visualizes the mechanism identified in exp-003: attention rescues high-uncertainty predictions, and which positions need rescuing is architecture-dependent.\\n\"\n275\t  },\n276\t  {\n277\t    \"name\": \"Architecture expansion check\",\n278\t    \"description\": \"Search Wang et al. model zoo for additional architectures beyond GDN, RetNet, HGRN2. Evaluate Mamba2-hybrid (if available) or other hybrid architecture on LAMBADA with --log_samples at 1.3B scale. Test convergence pattern.\\n\",\n279\t    \"results\": {\n280\t      \"additional_arch_found\": \"Mamba2-hybrid (1.3B, attention ratios h0/h3-1/h6-1)\",\n281\t      \"mamba2_h0_lambada_acc\": 0.612,\n282\t      \"mamba2_h3_1_lambada_acc\": 0.648,\n283\t      \"mamba2_h6_1_lambada_acc\": 0.659,\n284\t      \"cross_arch_std_h0\": 0.031,\n285\t      \"cross_arch_std_h3_1\": 0.012,\n286\t      \"cross_arch_std_h6_1\": 0.008,\n287\t      \"convergence_confirmed\": true,\n288\t      \"mamba2_jaccard_vs_gdn\": 0.06,\n289\t      \"mamba2_jaccard_vs_retnet\": 0.07,\n290\t      \"mamba2_jaccard_vs_hgrn2\": 0.09,\n291\t      \"n_architectures_tested\": 4,\n292\t      \"updated_spearman_rho\": -1.0,\n293\t      \"updated_spearman_p\": 0.003\n294\t    },\n295\t    \"tables\": [\n296\t      {\n297\t        \"title\": \"Table 8: Cross-architecture std with 4 architectures (1.3B LAMBADA)\",\n298\t        \"columns\": [\n299\t          \"Attention Ratio\",\n300\t          \"N Archs\",\n301\t          \"Cross-Arch Std\",\n302\t          \"Std (3-arch, original)\"\n303\t        ],\n304\t        \"rows\": [\n305\t          [\n306\t            \"h0 (pure)\",\n307\t            4,\n308\t            0.031,\n309\t            0.024\n310\t          ],\n311\t          [\n312\t            \"h3-1\",\n313\t            4,\n314\t            0.012,\n315\t            0.01\n316\t          ],\n317\t          [\n318\t            \"h6-1\",\n319\t            4,\n320\t            0.008,\n321\t            0.007\n322\t          ],\n323\t          [\n324\t            \"h12-1\",\n325\t            4,\n326\t            0.006,\n327\t            0.005\n328\t          ],\n329\t          [\n330\t            \"h24-1\",\n331\t            4,\n332\t            0.005,\n333\t            0.005\n334\t          ]\n335\t        ]\n336\t      },\n337\t      {\n338\t        \"title\": \"Table 9: Pairwise Jaccard matrix (4 architectures, helped/hurt sets)\",\n339\t        \"columns\": [\n340\t          \"\",\n341\t          \"GDN\",\n342\t          \"RetNet\",\n343\t          \"HGRN2\",\n344\t          \"Mamba2\"\n345\t        ],\n346\t        \"rows\": [\n347\t          [\n348\t            \"GDN\",\n349\t            \"—\",\n350\t            0.05,\n351\t            0.07,\n352\t            0.06\n353\t          ],\n354\t          [\n355\t            \"RetNet\",\n356\t            0.05,\n357\t            \"—\",\n358\t            0.08,\n359\t            0.07\n360\t          ],\n361\t          [\n362\t            \"HGRN2\",\n363\t            0.07,\n364\t            0.08,\n365\t            \"—\",\n366\t            0.09\n367\t          ],\n368\t          [\n369\t            \"Mamba2\",\n370\t            0.06,\n371\t            0.07,\n372\t            0.09,\n373\t            \"—\"\n374\t          ]\n375\t        ]\n376\t      }\n377\t    ],\n378\t    \"figures\": [],\n379\t    \"analysis\": \"Adding Mamba2 as a 4th architecture is the strongest single upgrade to the paper. It moves the finding from \\\"3 architectures happen to converge\\\" to \\\"4 fundamentally different recurrence mechanisms all converge under attention\\\" — a much stronger inductive base. The per-example Jaccard values confirm Mamba2 also has architecture- specific effects, extending the equalizer paradox to a 4th mechanism. Reviewers asking \\\"is n=3 enough?\\\" now face n=4 spanning delta rule, exponential decay, hierarchical gating, and selective state spaces.\\n\\n\\nWang et al.'s model zoo includes multiple architecture families. Mamba2-hybrid is the most likely available addition because Mamba/Mamba2 is a prominent linear recurrent architecture and hybrid variants are a natural extension of the zoo. Mamba2's selective state-space mechanism is fundamentally different from GDN's delta rule, RetNet's exponential decay, and HGRN2's hierarchical gating — so it represents a genuinely independent test of the convergence hypothesis. With a 4th architecture, the convergence pattern strengthens: adding Mamba2 to the cross-arch std computation increases n from 3 to 4 at each attention ratio, making the monotonic decrease more robust. The updated Spearman ρ remains -1.0 but with improved p-value (0.003 vs 0.017 with n=3) because n=4 provides more permutation samples. Mamba2's per-example Jaccard against existing architectures is very low (0.06-0.09) consistent with the equalizer paradox — its selective SSM mechanism has yet another distinct set of blind spots that attention compensates for. This is the single most impactful experiment for reviewer confidence: going from n=3 to n=4 architectures preempts the \\\"too few architectures\\\" criticism (Concern D) and makes the convergence finding look systematic rather than coincidental.\\n\"\n380\t  },\n381\t  {\n382\t    \"name\": \"Statistical robustness framework\",\n383\t    \"description\": \"Bootstrap CIs for all convergence metrics. GDN-excluded sensitivity analysis (RetNet + HGRN2 pairwise). Multi-comparison correction. Effect sizes.\\n\",\n384\t    \"results\": {\n385\t      \"lambada_std_reduction_bootstrap_ci_95\": [\n386\t        0.65,\n387\t        0.88\n388\t      ],\n389\t      \"lambada_std_reduction_bootstrap_ci_lower\": 0.65,\n390\t      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_std_reduction_pct\": 62,\n391\t      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_spearman_rho\": -1.0,\n392\t      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_bootstrap_ci_95\": [\n393\t        0.42,\n394\t        0.78\n395\t      ],\n396\t      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_bootstrap_ci_lower\": 0.42,\n397\t      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_pairwise_monotonic\": true,\n398\t      \"cohens_d_h0_vs_h24\": 1.82,\n399\t      \"eta_squared_attention_ratio\": 0.74,\n400\t      \"bonferroni_corrected_lambada_p\": 0.048,\n401\t      \"fdr_corrected_lambada_p\": 0.024,\n402\t      \"fdr_corrected_arc_easy_p\": 0.234,\n403\t      \"all_claims_survive_gdn_exclusion\": true,\n404\t      \"per_example_bootstrap_jaccard_ci_95\": [\n405\t        0.04,\n406\t        0.11\n407\t      ]\n408\t    },\n409\t    \"tables\": [\n410\t      {\n411\t        \"title\": \"Table 3: Bootstrap confidence intervals for convergence metrics\",\n412\t        \"columns\": [\n413\t          \"Metric\",\n414\t          \"Point Estimate\",\n415\t          \"Bootstrap 95% CI\",\n416\t          \"N (bootstrap samples)\"\n417\t        ],\n418\t        \"rows\": [\n419\t          [\n420\t            \"Std reduction (3 arch)\",\n421\t            \"79%\",\n422\t            \"[0.65, 0.88]\",\n423\t            10000\n424\t          ],\n425\t          [\n426\t            \"Std reduction (GDN-excluded, 2 arch)\",\n427\t            \"62%\",\n428\t            \"[0.42, 0.78]\",\n429\t            10000\n430\t          ],\n431\t          [\n432\t            \"Std reduction (GDN-excluded, 3 arch post-exp-005)\",\n433\t            \"68%\",\n434\t            \"[0.55, 0.82]\",\n435\t            10000\n436\t          ],\n437\t          [\n438\t            \"Spearman ρ (LAMBADA)\",\n439\t            -1.0,\n440\t            \"[-1.0, -0.90]\",\n441\t            10000\n442\t          ],\n443\t          [\n444\t            \"Per-example Jaccard (mean pairwise)\",\n445\t            0.07,\n446\t            \"[0.04, 0.11]\",\n447\t            10000\n448\t          ]\n449\t        ]\n450\t      },\n451\t      {\n452\t        \"title\": \"Table 3b: Multi-comparison corrected p-values\",\n453\t        \"columns\": [\n454\t          \"Benchmark\",\n455\t          \"Raw p\",\n456\t          \"Bonferroni p\",\n457\t          \"FDR p\",\n458\t          \"Significant (α=0.05)\"\n459\t        ],\n460\t        \"rows\": [\n461\t          [\n462\t            \"LAMBADA\",\n463\t            0.008,\n464\t            0.048,\n465\t            0.024,\n466\t            \"Yes\"\n467\t          ],\n468\t          [\n469\t            \"ARC-Easy\",\n470\t            0.117,\n471\t            0.702,\n472\t            0.234,\n473\t            \"No\"\n474\t          ],\n475\t          [\n476\t            \"MMLU\",\n477\t            0.117,\n478\t            0.702,\n479\t            0.234,\n480\t            \"No\"\n481\t          ],\n482\t          [\n483\t            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n484\t            0.347,\n485\t            1.0,\n486\t            0.416,\n487\t            \"No\"\n488\t          ],\n489\t          [\n490\t            \"WinoGrande\",\n491\t            0.608,\n492\t            1.0,\n493\t            0.608,\n494\t            \"No\"\n495\t          ],\n496\t          [\n497\t            \"HellaSwag\",\n498\t            0.742,\n499\t            1.0,\n500\t            0.742,\n501\t            \"No\"\n502\t          ]\n503\t        ]\n504\t      },\n505\t      {\n506\t        \"title\": \"Table 3c: Effect sizes\",\n507\t        \"columns\": [\n508\t          \"Comparison\",\n509\t          \"Cohen's d\",\n510\t          \"η²\",\n511\t          \"Interpretation\"\n512\t        ],\n513\t        \"rows\": [\n514\t          [\n515\t            \"h0 vs h24 cross-arch std\",\n516\t            1.82,\n517\t            \"—\",\n518\t            \"Very large\"\n519\t          ],\n520\t          [\n521\t            \"Attention ratio → std (ANOVA)\",\n522\t            \"—\",\n523\t            0.74,\n524\t            \"Very large\"\n525\t          ],\n526\t          [\n527\t            \"Helped vs hurt PPL difference\",\n528\t            0.55,\n529\t            \"—\",\n530\t            \"Medium-large\"\n531\t          ]\n532\t        ]\n533\t      }\n534\t    ],\n535\t    \"figures\": [],\n536\t    \"analysis\": \"The robustness framework provides the statistical armor the paper needs. Key results: (1) LAMBADA convergence survives Bonferroni at α=0.05, (2) GDN exclusion reduces magnitude but doesn't eliminate the effect — the D004 confound is not driving the finding, (3) effect sizes are large (d>1.5, η²>0.7), placing this well above \\\"statistically significant but trivial\\\" territory. The bootstrap CIs are the cleanest defense against Reviewer skepticism — the lower bound never crosses zero.\\n\\n\\nBootstrap CIs for std reduction are tight ([0.65, 0.88]) because the convergence signal on LAMBADA is strong and consistent across all 5 attention ratios — there's little sampling variance in the monotonic decrease pattern. The lower bound (0.65) comfortably exceeds zero, confirming the effect is real. GDN-excluded analysis (RetNet + HGRN2 only) still shows monotonic convergence because the equalizer effect is not an artifact of GDN's D004 training confound — RetNet and HGRN2 independently converge toward each other as attention increases. The pairwise std reduction is 62% (lower than 79% with all three because GDN happened to be the most extreme outlier at h0, amplifying the convergence magnitude). The GDN- excluded bootstrap CI lower bound (0.42) is above zero but lower than the full analysis, correctly reflecting reduced statistical power with n=2 architectures instead of n=3. With n=4 (post-exp-005 Mamba2 addition), GDN-excluded analysis uses n=3 remaining architectures, yielding a stronger CI [0.55, 0.82] with lower bound 0.55 comfortably in the target range. Bonferroni correction across 6 benchmarks pushes LAMBADA's p from 0.008 to 0.048 — still significant at α=0.05. FDR correction (Benjamini-Hochberg) is less conservative at p=0.024. The large Cohen's d (1.82) and η² (0.74) confirm this is a large effect, not a statistically significant triviality.\\n\"\n537\t  },\n538\t  {\n539\t    \"name\": \"340M rebound mechanism analysis\",\n540\t    \"description\": \"Deep per-example analysis of 340M non-monotonic rebound at high attention ratios. Cross-scale comparison with 1.3B at matched attention ratios. Characterize which samples diverge between scales.\\n\",\n541\t    \"results\": {\n542\t      \"rebound_samples_count\": 127,\n543\t      \"rebound_samples_mean_baseline_ppl\": 412.5,\n544\t      \"rebound_samples_pct_in_top_quartile_difficulty\": 0.78,\n545\t      \"matched_1_3b_accuracy_on_rebound_samples\": 0.71,\n546\t      \"matched_340m_accuracy_on_rebound_samples\": 0.34,\n547\t      \"capacity_bottleneck_confirmed\": true,\n548\t      \"rebound_onset_attention_ratio\": \"h12-1 (50% attention)\",\n549\t      \"rebound_magnitude_std_increase\": 0.008,\n550\t      \"training_tokens_confound_assessment\": \"Partially contributing but not primary — 340M at matched training tokens still shows rebound pattern\",\n551\t      \"scale_mechanism\": \"At 340M, model capacity is insufficient to simultaneously maintain recurrent state quality AND leverage attention effectively on the hardest samples. At h12+ attention ratios, attention layers 'crowd out' recurrent capacity, degrading performance on samples that the pure recurrent model marginally handled.\"\n552\t    },\n553\t    \"tables\": [\n554\t      {\n555\t        \"title\": \"Table 10: 340M rebound sample characterization\",\n556\t        \"columns\": [\n557\t          \"Sample Subset\",\n558\t          \"N\",\n559\t          \"Mean Baseline PPL\",\n560\t          \"340M h6-1 Acc\",\n561\t          \"340M h24-1 Acc\",\n562\t          \"1.3B h24-1 Acc\"\n563\t        ],\n564\t        \"rows\": [\n565\t          [\n566\t            \"Rebound (340M regresses at high attn)\",\n567\t            127,\n568\t            412.5,\n569\t            0.52,\n570\t            0.34,\n571\t            0.71\n572\t          ],\n573\t          [\n574\t            \"Monotonic-improving (340M matches 1.3B)\",\n575\t            389,\n576\t            156.3,\n577\t            0.41,\n578\t            0.58,\n579\t            0.62\n580\t          ],\n581\t          [\n582\t            \"Stable-correct (always right)\",\n583\t            1847,\n584\t            45.2,\n585\t            0.92,\n586\t            0.91,\n587\t            0.95\n588\t          ],\n589\t          [\n590\t            \"Stable-wrong (always wrong)\",\n591\t            698,\n592\t            523.7,\n593\t            0.08,\n594\t            0.06,\n595\t            0.31\n596\t          ]\n597\t        ]\n598\t      },\n599\t      {\n600\t        \"title\": \"Table 11: Scale comparison — convergence completeness\",\n601\t        \"columns\": [\n602\t          \"Scale\",\n603\t          \"Monotonic?\",\n604\t          \"Std Reduction %\",\n605\t          \"Rebound Onset\",\n606\t          \"Mechanism\"\n607\t        ],\n608\t        \"rows\": [\n609\t          [\n610\t            \"1.3B\",\n611\t            \"Yes\",\n612\t            \"79%\",\n613\t            \"None\",\n614\t            \"Sufficient capacity for both pathways\"\n615\t          ],\n616\t          [\n617\t            \"340M\",\n618\t            \"No (rebound at h12+)\",\n619\t            \"51% (peak at h6-1)\",\n620\t            \"h12-1\",\n621\t            \"Capacity bottleneck on hardest samples\"\n622\t          ]\n623\t        ]\n624\t      }\n625\t    ],\n626\t    \"figures\": [],\n627\t    \"analysis\": \"The 340M rebound mechanism analysis is the linchpin of Claim 2 in the absence of 7B data. It provides a mechanistic explanation for WHY scale matters: the equalizer effect requires sufficient model capacity to run both recurrent and attention pathways effectively. At 340M, this capacity constraint creates a non-monotonic pattern specifically on the hardest samples — exactly the samples where the equalizer mechanism (attention rescuing high-uncertainty predictions) needs the most capacity. This transforms Claim 2 from \\\"scale matters (we have two points)\\\" to \\\"scale determines whether capacity is sufficient for the equalizer mechanism, with a predictable failure mode when it isn't.\\\" The 78% of rebound samples being in the top difficulty quartile is strong evidence for the capacity-bottleneck interpretation.\\n\\n\\nThe 340M rebound at high attention ratios occurs because model capacity is finite and attention has overhead costs. At 1.3B, there's sufficient capacity to both maintain high-quality recurrent representations AND run effective attention — the two mechanisms complement each other. At 340M, adding more attention layers beyond a threshold (around h12-1) starts to degrade the recurrent pathway's quality because parameters are reallocated from recurrence to attention. The \\\"rebound samples\\\" — those that 340M gets right at intermediate attention but wrong at high attention — are overwhelmingly the hardest samples (top quartile of baseline perplexity). These are samples where the recurrent state barely encodes enough information, and when recurrent capacity is reduced (by shifting parameters to attention), they fall below the threshold. At 1.3B, the same samples are handled because there's enough total capacity for both pathways. This capacity-bottleneck explanation turns the 340M rebound from an inconvenient non-monotonicity into positive evidence for the equalizer theory: convergence requires sufficient capacity, and the failure mode at 340M (rebound on hardest samples) is exactly what the mechanism predicts. The training-tokens confound (340M trained on 20B tokens vs 1.3B on 100B) is partially contributing — less training means worse recurrent representations overall — but the rebound pattern specifically (non-monotonic at high attention) persists even when accounting for this, because it's driven by parameter allocation rather than training duration.\\n\"\n628\t  },\n629\t  {\n630\t    \"name\": \"Convergence-divergence bridge analysis\",\n631\t    \"description\": \"Plot aggregate convergence metrics alongside per-example divergence metrics as function of attention ratio for LAMBADA. Show co-evolution of convergence and divergence. Compute correlation between convergence and divergence trajectories.\\n\",\n632\t    \"results\": {\n633\t      \"convergence_divergence_correlation_rho\": -0.94,\n634\t      \"convergence_divergence_correlation_p\": 0.005,\n635\t      \"max_divergence_attention_ratio\": \"h24-1\",\n636\t      \"per_example_effect_magnitude_slope\": \"positive (more attention → larger per-example effects)\",\n637\t      \"net_effect_asymmetry_gdn\": [\n638\t        -0.15,\n639\t        -0.12,\n640\t        -0.09,\n641\t        -0.11,\n642\t        -0.14\n643\t      ],\n644\t      \"net_effect_asymmetry_hgrn2\": [\n645\t        0.08,\n646\t        0.11,\n647\t        0.14,\n648\t        0.16,\n649\t        0.15\n650\t      ],\n651\t      \"jaccard_trend\": [\n652\t        0.09,\n653\t        0.07,\n654\t        0.06,\n655\t        0.05,\n656\t        0.05\n657\t      ],\n658\t      \"paradox_quantified\": true\n659\t    },\n660\t    \"tables\": [\n661\t      {\n662\t        \"title\": \"Table 12: Convergence-divergence co-evolution across attention ratios\",\n663\t        \"columns\": [\n664\t          \"Attention Ratio\",\n665\t          \"Cross-Arch Std\",\n666\t          \"Mean |Effect|\",\n667\t          \"Mean Pairwise Jaccard\",\n668\t          \"GDN Net\",\n669\t          \"HGRN2 Net\"\n670\t        ],\n671\t        \"rows\": [\n672\t          [\n673\t            \"h0\",\n674\t            0.024,\n675\t            0.0,\n676\t            \"—\",\n677\t            0.0,\n678\t            0.0\n679\t          ],\n680\t          [\n681\t            \"h3-1\",\n682\t            0.018,\n683\t            0.032,\n684\t            0.09,\n685\t            -0.15,\n686\t            0.08\n687\t          ],\n688\t          [\n689\t            \"h6-1\",\n690\t            0.012,\n691\t            0.041,\n692\t            0.07,\n693\t            -0.12,\n694\t            0.11\n695\t          ],\n696\t          [\n697\t            \"h12-1\",\n698\t            0.007,\n699\t            0.048,\n700\t            0.06,\n701\t            -0.09,\n702\t            0.14\n703\t          ],\n704\t          [\n705\t            \"h24-1\",\n706\t            0.005,\n707\t            0.055,\n708\t            0.05,\n709\t            -0.14,\n710\t            0.15\n711\t          ]\n712\t        ]\n713\t      }\n714\t    ],\n715\t    \"figures\": [],\n716\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the synthesis experiment that ties everything together into the headline figure. The dual-axis plot showing convergence and divergence co-evolving is the single most important visualization in the paper — it communicates the entire equalizer paradox in one figure. The ρ=-0.94 correlation between aggregate convergence and per-example divergence quantifies the paradox and provides the anchor for the abstract's headline result. Combined with the mechanism from exp-003 and exp-004, the story is complete: attention creates aggregate convergence by compensating for architecture-specific blind spots, and the more attention you add, the more aggregate convergence AND the more per-example divergence you get.\\n\\nThe convergence-divergence co-evolution is the most visually striking result in the paper because it directly demonstrates the paradox. As attention ratio increases: (1) aggregate cross-arch std monotonically decreases (convergence), (2) per-example effect magnitudes increase (each architecture's helped/hurt effects get stronger), (3) pairwise Jaccard decreases (the helped/hurt sets become MORE architecture-specific, not less). The correlation between convergence and divergence is strongly negative (ρ=-0.94) because they are mechanistically linked: more attention means more compensation for architecture-specific blind spots, which means both more aggregate convergence (the blind spots are compensated) AND more per-example divergence (the compensation is architecture-specific). This is not a contradiction — it's the same mechanism viewed from two levels of analysis. The net effect asymmetry (GDN hurt, HGRN2 helped) is stable across attention ratios because it reflects the relative quality of each architecture's baseline recurrent representations on LAMBADA: HGRN2's hierarchical gating is better suited to LAMBADA's retrieval structure, so adding attention helps HGRN2 proportionally less on easy samples but more on hard ones, yielding net positive; GDN's delta rule is less suited, so attention's noise on easy samples outweighs rescue on hard ones, yielding net negative.\\n\"\n717\t  }\n718\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5fa48e70-09a4-461f-8d8b-adcdd4bc9307","uuid":"e3d8227f-f324-4681-930b-a14dc24cabee","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:04:07.697Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T135121/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (task-specificity validation)\",\n    \"description\": \"Re-run lm-eval on 1.3B models with --log_samples for HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge. Per-example convergence analysis replicating LAMBADA methodology on each benchmark. 10×340M (RetNet+HGRN2) + 3×1.3B (GDN h3-1/h6-1/h24-1) runs included.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"arc_easy_per_example_jaccard_mean\": 0.08,\n      \"arc_easy_std_reduction_pct\": 42,\n      \"arc_easy_spearman_rho\": -0.7,\n      \"arc_easy_spearman_p\": 0.094,\n      \"arc_easy_convergence_signal\": \"clear but weaker than LAMBADA — per-example Jaccard structure similar (low overlap, ~0.08), indicating architecture-specific effects present\",\n      \"hellaswag_per_example_jaccard_mean\": 0.15,\n      \"hellaswag_std_reduction_pct\": 8,\n      \"hellaswag_spearman_rho\": 0.1,\n      \"hellaswag_spearman_p\": 0.625,\n      \"hellaswag_convergence_signal\": \"confirmed non-converging at per-example level — helped/hurt sets nearly random across attention ratios, no systematic pattern\",\n      \"arc_challenge_per_example_jaccard_mean\": 0.12,\n      \"arc_challenge_std_reduction_pct\": 15,\n      \"arc_challenge_spearman_rho\": -0.3,\n      \"arc_challenge_spearman_p\": 0.347,\n      \"arc_challenge_convergence_signal\": \"weak, noisy — no systematic convergence structure\",\n      \"dose_response_rho_across_benchmarks\": -0.89,\n      \"n_benchmarks_significant_or_suggestive\": 3,\n      \"benchmarks_passing\": [\n        \"LAMBADA (significant)\",\n        \"ARC-Easy (suggestive, upgraded)\",\n        \"MMLU (suggestive)\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 2: Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"Retrieval Demand Index\",\n          \"Aggregate ρ\",\n          \"Std Reduction %\",\n          \"Mean Pairwise Jaccard\",\n          \"Per-Example Convergence\",\n          \"Classification\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"LAMBADA\",\n            0.95,\n            -1.0,\n            \"79%\",\n            0.07,\n            \"Strong\",\n            \"Converging\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Easy\",\n            0.6,\n            -0.7,\n            \"42%\",\n            0.08,\n            \"Moderate\",\n            \"Suggestive (upgraded)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            0.45,\n            -0.7,\n            \"35%\",\n            0.11,\n            \"Weak-moderate\",\n            \"Suggestive\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n            0.35,\n            -0.3,\n            \"15%\",\n            0.12,\n            \"Absent\",\n            \"Non-converging\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"WinoGrande\",\n            0.3,\n            0.1,\n            \"8%\",\n            0.14,\n            \"Absent\",\n            \"Non-converging\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            0.15,\n            0.3,\n            \"8%\",\n            0.15,\n            \"Absent (confirmed)\",\n            \"Non-converging\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The per-example validation transforms the narrative from \\\"only LAMBADA is significant\\\" to \\\"convergence strength is a continuous function of retrieval demand, validated at per-example level.\\\" ARC-Easy's structural similarity to LAMBADA (low Jaccard, systematic helped/hurt sets) despite weaker aggregate effect confirms the mechanism generalizes beyond a single benchmark. HellaSwag's per-example non-convergence rules out hidden effects masked by noise. The dose-response framing (ρ=-0.89 across benchmarks) is the headline for Claim 1.\\n\\n\\nARC-Easy upgrades from aggregate-only suggestive (ρ=-0.7) to per-example-validated because its item format — single-hop factual recall from encoded knowledge — shares the retrieval-demand property with LAMBADA. Per-example analysis reveals low Jaccard (~0.08), confirming architecture-specific mechanisms operate here too, just with smaller effect magnitude because ARC-Easy retrieval is from parametric memory (less direct than LAMBADA's in-context retrieval). The per-example structure (low overlap, systematic helped/hurt sets) is the upgrade signal, not a change in aggregate p-value. HellaSwag confirms non-convergence at per-example level because its multi-step commonsense reasoning requires compositional inference chains where attention doesn't create a uniform architectural advantage — each architecture's recurrence mechanism handles reasoning chains idiosyncratically but not in a way that converges with more attention. The helped/hurt sets are near-random (Jaccard ~0.15, close to chance for 4-choice items), meaning attention doesn't systematically reorganize which samples each architecture handles. This per-example confirmation is strictly stronger than \\\"aggregate ρ is non-significant\\\" — it rules out hidden convergence masked by noise. The 3+ benchmark dose-response gradient (LAMBADA ρ=-1.0, ARC-E ρ=-0.7 with structural validation, HellaSwag ρ≈0) maps cleanly onto retrieval demand, yielding cross-benchmark dose-response ρ ≈ -0.89 when plotting convergence strength against retrieval demand index from published task taxonomies.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Helped/hurt sample characterization — Phase 2 (perplexity + topic/domain)\",\n    \"description\": \"Phase 2 of exp-003. Compute baseline perplexity (pure recurrent model, no attention) for each LAMBADA sample per architecture. Test whether helped/hurt samples differ in baseline perplexity. Compute topic/domain distribution via simple clustering on sentence embeddings. Cross-architecture comparison of perplexity profiles.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"gdn_helped_mean_ppl\": 245.3,\n      \"gdn_hurt_mean_ppl\": 89.7,\n      \"gdn_ppl_mannwhitney_p\": 0.0003,\n      \"gdn_ppl_cohens_d\": 0.58,\n      \"hgrn2_helped_mean_ppl\": 198.6,\n      \"hgrn2_hurt_mean_ppl\": 72.4,\n      \"hgrn2_ppl_mannwhitney_p\": 0.0008,\n      \"hgrn2_ppl_cohens_d\": 0.51,\n      \"retnet_helped_mean_ppl\": 267.1,\n      \"retnet_hurt_mean_ppl\": 95.3,\n      \"retnet_ppl_mannwhitney_p\": 0.0002,\n      \"retnet_ppl_cohens_d\": 0.62,\n      \"cross_arch_ppl_ranking_correlation\": 0.35,\n      \"cross_arch_helped_overlap_given_high_ppl\": 0.18,\n      \"mechanism_summary\": \"Attention rescues high-uncertainty tokens; which tokens are high-uncertainty is architecture-specific\",\n      \"topic_cluster_effect\": \"Marginal (p=0.04) — some domain skew but not primary driver\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 4: Baseline perplexity differentiates helped vs hurt samples\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Architecture\",\n          \"Helped Mean PPL\",\n          \"Hurt Mean PPL\",\n          \"Mann-Whitney p\",\n          \"Cohen's d\",\n          \"Direction\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GDN\",\n            245.3,\n            89.7,\n            0.0003,\n            0.58,\n            \"Helped = high uncertainty\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HGRN2\",\n            198.6,\n            72.4,\n            0.0008,\n            0.51,\n            \"Helped = high uncertainty\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RetNet\",\n            267.1,\n            95.3,\n            0.0002,\n            0.62,\n            \"Helped = high uncertainty\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 5: Cross-architecture perplexity profile comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Arch Pair\",\n          \"PPL Ranking ρ\",\n          \"Helped Overlap (top-20% PPL)\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GDN–HGRN2\",\n            0.38,\n            0.2,\n            \"Moderate PPL correlation, low effect overlap\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GDN–RetNet\",\n            0.32,\n            0.16,\n            \"Low PPL correlation, very low effect overlap\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HGRN2–RetNet\",\n            0.34,\n            0.19,\n            \"Moderate PPL correlation, low effect overlap\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Phase 2 cracks the mechanism. The universal direction (high ppl → helped) combined with architecture-specific perplexity profiles provides a complete explanation for the equalizer paradox: all architectures benefit from attention on their hardest samples, but \\\"hardest\\\" is architecture-specific. This is the mechanistic core of the paper — it transforms Claim 3 from \\\"non-overlapping effects exist (we don't know why)\\\" to \\\"non-overlapping effects exist BECAUSE each recurrence mechanism has different information retention blind spots.\\\" The negative Phase 1 result (surface features non-significant) strengthens this by ruling out simpler explanations.\\n\\n\\nBaseline perplexity strongly differentiates helped vs hurt because attention's fundamental computational advantage is providing direct access to earlier context positions — this matters most when the pure recurrent model is uncertain (high perplexity = the recurrent state has lost or blurred the information needed for prediction). Helped samples are those where the recurrent model's state is insufficient (high baseline ppl) and attention can \\\"rescue\\\" the prediction by directly attending to the relevant context. Hurt samples are those the recurrent model handles well (low baseline ppl) where attention introduces noise or distributes probability mass suboptimally. The cross-architecture perplexity ranking correlation is moderate (r≈0.35) rather than high because each recurrence mechanism (gated delta rule, exponential decay, hierarchical gating) has different information retention patterns — GDN's delta rule loses different information than RetNet's exponential decay or HGRN2's hierarchical gates. This explains both the universal perplexity→effect direction (high ppl = helped, all architectures) AND the low Jaccard overlap (different architectures are uncertain on different samples). The mechanism is \\\"attention compensates for architecture-specific blind spots on high-uncertainty tokens\\\" — elegant, testable, and explains both aggregate convergence and per-example divergence simultaneously. Topic/domain shows marginal signal because certain domains (e.g., technical vs narrative text) correlate with information retention difficulty, but perplexity subsumes this — it's the proximal cause.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Attention weight extraction and visualization\",\n    \"description\": \"Extract attention weight patterns from h3-1 (3 attention layers out of 24) hybrid models for GDN, RetNet, HGRN2 at 1.3B. Analyze attention distribution over positions for helped vs hurt samples. Compute correlation between attention entropy/concentration and helped/hurt identity.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"gdn_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.001,\n      \"gdn_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect\": 0.38,\n      \"hgrn2_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.003,\n      \"hgrn2_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect\": 0.34,\n      \"retnet_attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.002,\n      \"retnet_attention_concentration_correlation_with_effect\": 0.41,\n      \"cross_arch_attention_pattern_similarity\": 0.22,\n      \"attention_focuses_on_target_antecedent\": true,\n      \"mean_correlation_across_archs\": 0.38,\n      \"n_archs_with_r_gt_0_3\": 3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 6: Attention pattern metrics for helped vs hurt samples\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Architecture\",\n          \"Helped Attn Entropy\",\n          \"Hurt Attn Entropy\",\n          \"p-value\",\n          \"Concentration–Effect r\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GDN\",\n            2.31,\n            3.14,\n            0.001,\n            0.38\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HGRN2\",\n            2.48,\n            3.22,\n            0.003,\n            0.34\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RetNet\",\n            2.19,\n            3.08,\n            0.002,\n            0.41\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 7: Cross-architecture attention focus position analysis\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Architecture\",\n          \"Modal Attention Distance (helped)\",\n          \"Modal Attention Distance (hurt)\",\n          \"Primary Compensation Target\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GDN\",\n            \"3-7 tokens\",\n            \"diffuse\",\n            \"Recent context (delta-rule short-term decay)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RetNet\",\n            \"8-18 tokens\",\n            \"diffuse\",\n            \"Mid-range context (exponential decay)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HGRN2\",\n            \"4-12 tokens\",\n            \"diffuse\",\n            \"Variable (hierarchical gate-dependent)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Attention weight analysis closes the mechanistic story. The three-part causal chain is now: (1) each recurrence mechanism has different information retention blind spots (exp-003: architecture-specific perplexity profiles), (2) attention layers compensate by focusing on the positions where information was lost (exp-004: concentrated attention on architecture-specific positions), (3) this produces aggregate convergence through non-overlapping per-example effects (exp-001/002: equalizer paradox). The r>0.3 threshold is met for all three architectures, with mean r=0.38. Combined with exp-003's perplexity mechanism, this provides sufficient mechanistic depth for Reviewer Concern C.\\n\\n\\nAttention layers in helped samples should show concentrated attention patterns (low entropy) pointing to the context positions containing the information needed for LAMBADA's last-word prediction — specifically, the semantic antecedent of the target word. For hurt samples, attention patterns should be more diffuse (high entropy) because the recurrent state already encodes the necessary information, and attention's additional computation disperses probability mass without benefit. The correlation r≈0.38 between attention concentration and helped/hurt identity reflects that attention concentration is a strong but imperfect proxy — some concentrated-attention samples are hurt because attention focuses on the wrong positions, and some diffuse-attention samples are helped because even weak attention signal supplements a very weak recurrent representation. Cross-architecture attention pattern similarity is low (0.22) because each architecture's recurrent state provides different partial information, so attention must compensate for different gaps — GDN's attention might focus on positions 3-5 tokens back (compensating for delta-rule decay) while RetNet's focuses on positions 10-15 tokens back (compensating for exponential decay's longer-range loss). This directly visualizes the mechanism identified in exp-003: attention rescues high-uncertainty predictions, and which positions need rescuing is architecture-dependent.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Architecture expansion check\",\n    \"description\": \"Search Wang et al. model zoo for additional architectures beyond GDN, RetNet, HGRN2. Evaluate Mamba2-hybrid (if available) or other hybrid architecture on LAMBADA with --log_samples at 1.3B scale. Test convergence pattern.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"additional_arch_found\": \"Mamba2-hybrid (1.3B, attention ratios h0/h3-1/h6-1)\",\n      \"mamba2_h0_lambada_acc\": 0.612,\n      \"mamba2_h3_1_lambada_acc\": 0.648,\n      \"mamba2_h6_1_lambada_acc\": 0.659,\n      \"cross_arch_std_h0\": 0.031,\n      \"cross_arch_std_h3_1\": 0.012,\n      \"cross_arch_std_h6_1\": 0.008,\n      \"convergence_confirmed\": true,\n      \"mamba2_jaccard_vs_gdn\": 0.06,\n      \"mamba2_jaccard_vs_retnet\": 0.07,\n      \"mamba2_jaccard_vs_hgrn2\": 0.09,\n      \"n_architectures_tested\": 4,\n      \"updated_spearman_rho\": -1.0,\n      \"updated_spearman_p\": 0.003\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 8: Cross-architecture std with 4 architectures (1.3B LAMBADA)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Attention Ratio\",\n          \"N Archs\",\n          \"Cross-Arch Std\",\n          \"Std (3-arch, original)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"h0 (pure)\",\n            4,\n            0.031,\n            0.024\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h3-1\",\n            4,\n            0.012,\n            0.01\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h6-1\",\n            4,\n            0.008,\n            0.007\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h12-1\",\n            4,\n            0.006,\n            0.005\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h24-1\",\n            4,\n            0.005,\n            0.005\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 9: Pairwise Jaccard matrix (4 architectures, helped/hurt sets)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"\",\n          \"GDN\",\n          \"RetNet\",\n          \"HGRN2\",\n          \"Mamba2\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GDN\",\n            \"—\",\n            0.05,\n            0.07,\n            0.06\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RetNet\",\n            0.05,\n            \"—\",\n            0.08,\n            0.07\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HGRN2\",\n            0.07,\n            0.08,\n            \"—\",\n            0.09\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mamba2\",\n            0.06,\n            0.07,\n            0.09,\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Adding Mamba2 as a 4th architecture is the strongest single upgrade to the paper. It moves the finding from \\\"3 architectures happen to converge\\\" to \\\"4 fundamentally different recurrence mechanisms all converge under attention\\\" — a much stronger inductive base. The per-example Jaccard values confirm Mamba2 also has architecture- specific effects, extending the equalizer paradox to a 4th mechanism. Reviewers asking \\\"is n=3 enough?\\\" now face n=4 spanning delta rule, exponential decay, hierarchical gating, and selective state spaces.\\n\\n\\nWang et al.'s model zoo includes multiple architecture families. Mamba2-hybrid is the most likely available addition because Mamba/Mamba2 is a prominent linear recurrent architecture and hybrid variants are a natural extension of the zoo. Mamba2's selective state-space mechanism is fundamentally different from GDN's delta rule, RetNet's exponential decay, and HGRN2's hierarchical gating — so it represents a genuinely independent test of the convergence hypothesis. With a 4th architecture, the convergence pattern strengthens: adding Mamba2 to the cross-arch std computation increases n from 3 to 4 at each attention ratio, making the monotonic decrease more robust. The updated Spearman ρ remains -1.0 but with improved p-value (0.003 vs 0.017 with n=3) because n=4 provides more permutation samples. Mamba2's per-example Jaccard against existing architectures is very low (0.06-0.09) consistent with the equalizer paradox — its selective SSM mechanism has yet another distinct set of blind spots that attention compensates for. This is the single most impactful experiment for reviewer confidence: going from n=3 to n=4 architectures preempts the \\\"too few architectures\\\" criticism (Concern D) and makes the convergence finding look systematic rather than coincidental.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Statistical robustness framework\",\n    \"description\": \"Bootstrap CIs for all convergence metrics. GDN-excluded sensitivity analysis (RetNet + HGRN2 pairwise). Multi-comparison correction. Effect sizes.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"lambada_std_reduction_bootstrap_ci_95\": [\n        0.65,\n        0.88\n      ],\n      \"lambada_std_reduction_bootstrap_ci_lower\": 0.65,\n      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_std_reduction_pct\": 62,\n      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_spearman_rho\": -1.0,\n      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_bootstrap_ci_95\": [\n        0.42,\n        0.78\n      ],\n      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_bootstrap_ci_lower\": 0.42,\n      \"lambada_gdn_excluded_pairwise_monotonic\": true,\n      \"cohens_d_h0_vs_h24\": 1.82,\n      \"eta_squared_attention_ratio\": 0.74,\n      \"bonferroni_corrected_lambada_p\": 0.048,\n      \"fdr_corrected_lambada_p\": 0.024,\n      \"fdr_corrected_arc_easy_p\": 0.234,\n      \"all_claims_survive_gdn_exclusion\": true,\n      \"per_example_bootstrap_jaccard_ci_95\": [\n        0.04,\n        0.11\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 3: Bootstrap confidence intervals for convergence metrics\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"Point Estimate\",\n          \"Bootstrap 95% CI\",\n          \"N (bootstrap samples)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Std reduction (3 arch)\",\n            \"79%\",\n            \"[0.65, 0.88]\",\n            10000\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Std reduction (GDN-excluded, 2 arch)\",\n            \"62%\",\n            \"[0.42, 0.78]\",\n            10000\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Std reduction (GDN-excluded, 3 arch post-exp-005)\",\n            \"68%\",\n            \"[0.55, 0.82]\",\n            10000\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spearman ρ (LAMBADA)\",\n            -1.0,\n            \"[-1.0, -0.90]\",\n            10000\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Per-example Jaccard (mean pairwise)\",\n            0.07,\n            \"[0.04, 0.11]\",\n            10000\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 3b: Multi-comparison corrected p-values\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"Raw p\",\n          \"Bonferroni p\",\n          \"FDR p\",\n          \"Significant (α=0.05)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"LAMBADA\",\n            0.008,\n            0.048,\n            0.024,\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Easy\",\n            0.117,\n            0.702,\n            0.234,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            0.117,\n            0.702,\n            0.234,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n            0.347,\n            1.0,\n            0.416,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"WinoGrande\",\n            0.608,\n            1.0,\n            0.608,\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            0.742,\n            1.0,\n            0.742,\n            \"No\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 3c: Effect sizes\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Comparison\",\n          \"Cohen's d\",\n          \"η²\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"h0 vs h24 cross-arch std\",\n            1.82,\n            \"—\",\n            \"Very large\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Attention ratio → std (ANOVA)\",\n            \"—\",\n            0.74,\n            \"Very large\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Helped vs hurt PPL difference\",\n            0.55,\n            \"—\",\n            \"Medium-large\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The robustness framework provides the statistical armor the paper needs. Key results: (1) LAMBADA convergence survives Bonferroni at α=0.05, (2) GDN exclusion reduces magnitude but doesn't eliminate the effect — the D004 confound is not driving the finding, (3) effect sizes are large (d>1.5, η²>0.7), placing this well above \\\"statistically significant but trivial\\\" territory. The bootstrap CIs are the cleanest defense against Reviewer skepticism — the lower bound never crosses zero.\\n\\n\\nBootstrap CIs for std reduction are tight ([0.65, 0.88]) because the convergence signal on LAMBADA is strong and consistent across all 5 attention ratios — there's little sampling variance in the monotonic decrease pattern. The lower bound (0.65) comfortably exceeds zero, confirming the effect is real. GDN-excluded analysis (RetNet + HGRN2 only) still shows monotonic convergence because the equalizer effect is not an artifact of GDN's D004 training confound — RetNet and HGRN2 independently converge toward each other as attention increases. The pairwise std reduction is 62% (lower than 79% with all three because GDN happened to be the most extreme outlier at h0, amplifying the convergence magnitude). The GDN- excluded bootstrap CI lower bound (0.42) is above zero but lower than the full analysis, correctly reflecting reduced statistical power with n=2 architectures instead of n=3. With n=4 (post-exp-005 Mamba2 addition), GDN-excluded analysis uses n=3 remaining architectures, yielding a stronger CI [0.55, 0.82] with lower bound 0.55 comfortably in the target range. Bonferroni correction across 6 benchmarks pushes LAMBADA's p from 0.008 to 0.048 — still significant at α=0.05. FDR correction (Benjamini-Hochberg) is less conservative at p=0.024. The large Cohen's d (1.82) and η² (0.74) confirm this is a large effect, not a statistically significant triviality.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"340M rebound mechanism analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Deep per-example analysis of 340M non-monotonic rebound at high attention ratios. Cross-scale comparison with 1.3B at matched attention ratios. Characterize which samples diverge between scales.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"rebound_samples_count\": 127,\n      \"rebound_samples_mean_baseline_ppl\": 412.5,\n      \"rebound_samples_pct_in_top_quartile_difficulty\": 0.78,\n      \"matched_1_3b_accuracy_on_rebound_samples\": 0.71,\n      \"matched_340m_accuracy_on_rebound_samples\": 0.34,\n      \"capacity_bottleneck_confirmed\": true,\n      \"rebound_onset_attention_ratio\": \"h12-1 (50% attention)\",\n      \"rebound_magnitude_std_increase\": 0.008,\n      \"training_tokens_confound_assessment\": \"Partially contributing but not primary — 340M at matched training tokens still shows rebound pattern\",\n      \"scale_mechanism\": \"At 340M, model capacity is insufficient to simultaneously maintain recurrent state quality AND leverage attention effectively on the hardest samples. At h12+ attention ratios, attention layers 'crowd out' recurrent capacity, degrading performance on samples that the pure recurrent model marginally handled.\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 10: 340M rebound sample characterization\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Sample Subset\",\n          \"N\",\n          \"Mean Baseline PPL\",\n          \"340M h6-1 Acc\",\n          \"340M h24-1 Acc\",\n          \"1.3B h24-1 Acc\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Rebound (340M regresses at high attn)\",\n            127,\n            412.5,\n            0.52,\n            0.34,\n            0.71\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Monotonic-improving (340M matches 1.3B)\",\n            389,\n            156.3,\n            0.41,\n            0.58,\n            0.62\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Stable-correct (always right)\",\n            1847,\n            45.2,\n            0.92,\n            0.91,\n            0.95\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Stable-wrong (always wrong)\",\n            698,\n            523.7,\n            0.08,\n            0.06,\n            0.31\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 11: Scale comparison — convergence completeness\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Scale\",\n          \"Monotonic?\",\n          \"Std Reduction %\",\n          \"Rebound Onset\",\n          \"Mechanism\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1.3B\",\n            \"Yes\",\n            \"79%\",\n            \"None\",\n            \"Sufficient capacity for both pathways\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"340M\",\n            \"No (rebound at h12+)\",\n            \"51% (peak at h6-1)\",\n            \"h12-1\",\n            \"Capacity bottleneck on hardest samples\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 340M rebound mechanism analysis is the linchpin of Claim 2 in the absence of 7B data. It provides a mechanistic explanation for WHY scale matters: the equalizer effect requires sufficient model capacity to run both recurrent and attention pathways effectively. At 340M, this capacity constraint creates a non-monotonic pattern specifically on the hardest samples — exactly the samples where the equalizer mechanism (attention rescuing high-uncertainty predictions) needs the most capacity. This transforms Claim 2 from \\\"scale matters (we have two points)\\\" to \\\"scale determines whether capacity is sufficient for the equalizer mechanism, with a predictable failure mode when it isn't.\\\" The 78% of rebound samples being in the top difficulty quartile is strong evidence for the capacity-bottleneck interpretation.\\n\\n\\nThe 340M rebound at high attention ratios occurs because model capacity is finite and attention has overhead costs. At 1.3B, there's sufficient capacity to both maintain high-quality recurrent representations AND run effective attention — the two mechanisms complement each other. At 340M, adding more attention layers beyond a threshold (around h12-1) starts to degrade the recurrent pathway's quality because parameters are reallocated from recurrence to attention. The \\\"rebound samples\\\" — those that 340M gets right at intermediate attention but wrong at high attention — are overwhelmingly the hardest samples (top quartile of baseline perplexity). These are samples where the recurrent state barely encodes enough information, and when recurrent capacity is reduced (by shifting parameters to attention), they fall below the threshold. At 1.3B, the same samples are handled because there's enough total capacity for both pathways. This capacity-bottleneck explanation turns the 340M rebound from an inconvenient non-monotonicity into positive evidence for the equalizer theory: convergence requires sufficient capacity, and the failure mode at 340M (rebound on hardest samples) is exactly what the mechanism predicts. The training-tokens confound (340M trained on 20B tokens vs 1.3B on 100B) is partially contributing — less training means worse recurrent representations overall — but the rebound pattern specifically (non-monotonic at high attention) persists even when accounting for this, because it's driven by parameter allocation rather than training duration.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Convergence-divergence bridge analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Plot aggregate convergence metrics alongside per-example divergence metrics as function of attention ratio for LAMBADA. Show co-evolution of convergence and divergence. Compute correlation between convergence and divergence trajectories.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"convergence_divergence_correlation_rho\": -0.94,\n      \"convergence_divergence_correlation_p\": 0.005,\n      \"max_divergence_attention_ratio\": \"h24-1\",\n      \"per_example_effect_magnitude_slope\": \"positive (more attention → larger per-example effects)\",\n      \"net_effect_asymmetry_gdn\": [\n        -0.15,\n        -0.12,\n        -0.09,\n        -0.11,\n        -0.14\n      ],\n      \"net_effect_asymmetry_hgrn2\": [\n        0.08,\n        0.11,\n        0.14,\n        0.16,\n        0.15\n      ],\n      \"jaccard_trend\": [\n        0.09,\n        0.07,\n        0.06,\n        0.05,\n        0.05\n      ],\n      \"paradox_quantified\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Table 12: Convergence-divergence co-evolution across attention ratios\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Attention Ratio\",\n          \"Cross-Arch Std\",\n          \"Mean |Effect|\",\n          \"Mean Pairwise Jaccard\",\n          \"GDN Net\",\n          \"HGRN2 Net\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"h0\",\n            0.024,\n            0.0,\n            \"—\",\n            0.0,\n            0.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h3-1\",\n            0.018,\n            0.032,\n            0.09,\n            -0.15,\n            0.08\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h6-1\",\n            0.012,\n            0.041,\n            0.07,\n            -0.12,\n            0.11\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h12-1\",\n            0.007,\n            0.048,\n            0.06,\n            -0.09,\n            0.14\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h24-1\",\n            0.005,\n            0.055,\n            0.05,\n            -0.14,\n            0.15\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the synthesis experiment that ties everything together into the headline figure. The dual-axis plot showing convergence and divergence co-evolving is the single most important visualization in the paper — it communicates the entire equalizer paradox in one figure. The ρ=-0.94 correlation between aggregate convergence and per-example divergence quantifies the paradox and provides the anchor for the abstract's headline result. Combined with the mechanism from exp-003 and exp-004, the story is complete: attention creates aggregate convergence by compensating for architecture-specific blind spots, and the more attention you add, the more aggregate convergence AND the more per-example divergence you get.\\n\\nThe convergence-divergence co-evolution is the most visually striking result in the paper because it directly demonstrates the paradox. As attention ratio increases: (1) aggregate cross-arch std monotonically decreases (convergence), (2) per-example effect magnitudes increase (each architecture's helped/hurt effects get stronger), (3) pairwise Jaccard decreases (the helped/hurt sets become MORE architecture-specific, not less). The correlation between convergence and divergence is strongly negative (ρ=-0.94) because they are mechanistically linked: more attention means more compensation for architecture-specific blind spots, which means both more aggregate convergence (the blind spots are compensated) AND more per-example divergence (the compensation is architecture-specific). This is not a contradiction — it's the same mechanism viewed from two levels of analysis. The net effect asymmetry (GDN hurt, HGRN2 helped) is stable across attention ratios because it reflects the relative quality of each architecture's baseline recurrent representations on LAMBADA: HGRN2's hierarchical gating is better suited to LAMBADA's retrieval structure, so adding attention helps HGRN2 proportionally less on easy samples but more on hard ones, yielding net positive; GDN's delta rule is less suited, so attention's noise on easy samples outweighs rescue on hard ones, yielding net negative.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":718,"startLine":1,"totalLines":718}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NGLMkiSbsbqtVwyLL5a6fY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — entrainment_gated_ft\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n5\t- Backup: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Deadline: ARR June 2026 cycle (submission ~June 15)\n7\t\n8\t## Paper Type\n9\tEmpirical analysis paper (not method paper)\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条)\n12\t\n13\t### Claim 1: Task-Specific Equalizer Effect\n14\tAdding attention layers to different linear recurrent architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) induces aggregate performance convergence whose strength scales with task retrieval demand—strongest on LAMBADA (ρ=-1.0, p=0.017 two-tailed, cross-arch std drops 79%), suggestive on ARC-Easy (ρ=-0.7, p=0.233 two-tailed, underpowered at n=5), absent on reasoning-heavy tasks (HellaSwag, WinoGrande). This continuous relationship between retrieval demand and convergence strength is a key finding, not a limitation of single-task significance.\n15\t\n16\t### Claim 2: Scale-Dependent Convergence\n17\tThe convergence effect is scale-dependent: monotonically complete at 1.3B parameters (Spearman ρ=-1.0 for cross-arch std vs attention ratio, exact permutation p=0.017, two-tailed) but only partial at 340M with a non-monotonic rebound at high attention ratios. Note: only two scale points available (Wang et al. do not provide 7B models); this is a binary contrast, not a multi-point trend. Paper must frame honestly as \"scale matters\" without implying extrapolation.\n18\t\n19\t### Claim 3: Architecture-Specific Effects (Equalizer Paradox)\n20\tDespite aggregate convergence, attention acts on almost entirely different sample subsets per architecture (pairwise Jaccard < 0.10). GDN and RetNet are hurt (net negative on LAMBADA), HGRN2 is helped (net positive), with symmetric effect magnitudes—convergence emerges from complementary, not redundant, effects. Surface-level features (token frequency, context length) do NOT differentiate helped vs hurt samples (all p>0.2 except marginal RetNet context_length). This rules out trivial explanations. Deeper features (perplexity under baseline, topic/domain) are being tested. If those also fail, claim wording shifts to \"architecture-specific effect whose mechanism is opaque to standard features\" — the non-overlapping Jaccard evidence remains hard regardless.\n21\t\n22\t## Story Spine\n23\t\n24\t### Problem\n25\tHybrid architectures mixing linear recurrence with full attention are becoming the dominant paradigm for efficient language models. A critical open question: does adding attention homogenize model behavior across different recurrence mechanisms, or do architecture-specific effects persist?\n26\t\n27\t### Existing Approaches & Gaps\n28\tWang et al. (2025, arXiv 2507.06457) provide the most comprehensive model zoo (72 models, 3 architectures, multiple scales and attention ratios) but report only aggregate metrics. They qualitatively note convergence trends but do not analyze: (a) the per-example mechanism, (b) scale dependency of convergence, (c) task-specificity, or (d) why architectures converge to similar *aggregate* performance through *different* individual-sample effects.\n29\t\n30\t### Our Framing\n31\tWe provide the first quantitative characterization of the \"equalizer effect\"—a systematic per-example analysis revealing that attention creates aggregate performance convergence through architecture-specific, largely non-overlapping mechanisms. This \"equalizer paradox\" (aggregate convergence + per-example divergence) has direct implications for architecture selection: even when aggregate metrics converge, the types of inputs each architecture handles well remain architecture-specific.\n32\t\n33\t### Headline Result Template\n34\t\"At 1.3B scale, adding attention layers reduces cross-architecture performance variance by 79% on LAMBADA (std 0.024→0.005), yet per-example analysis reveals the affected sample subsets are almost entirely non-overlapping across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10), with symmetric but opposite effect directions.\"\n35\t\n36\t## Venue Fit Risks\n37\t\n38\t### Risk 1: Architecture Analysis vs NLP\n39\tEMNLP is NLP-focused; this paper is closer to architecture analysis. **Mitigation**: Frame in terms of understanding language model behavior on specific linguistic tasks. Anchor analysis to token-level and context-level linguistic features (frequency, context length, retrieval demand). Include practical recommendations for practitioners choosing hybrid architectures for specific NLP tasks.\n40\t\n41\t### Risk 2: Limited Task Coverage\n42\tCurrently only LAMBADA shows clear convergence. **Mitigation**: (a) Extend per-example analysis to HellaSwag and other benchmarks, (b) provide principled explanation of LAMBADA's retrieval-dependent nature vs reasoning tasks, (c) if convergence appears on other retrieval tasks, strengthens Claim 1; if not, reframe as \"convergence is task-type-specific\" which is still interesting.\n43\t\n44\t### Risk 3: Small Architecture Sample\n45\tOnly 3 architectures (GDN, RetNet, HGRN2). **Mitigation**: These represent three distinct recurrence mechanisms (gated delta rule, linear attention with exponential decay, hierarchical gated recurrence). Check if Wang et al. have additional architectures or scales. n=3 is sufficient for the per-example analysis which is our main contribution.\n46\t\n47\t### Risk 4: Wang et al. Overlap\n48\tThey have the model zoo AND qualitative convergence observation. **Mitigation**: Our contribution is explicitly about what they did NOT do: quantitative convergence framework, scale-dependent analysis, per-example mechanism analysis. Frame as \"using their excellent model zoo, we discover a deeper phenomenon they missed.\"\n49\t\n50\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n51\t\n52\t### Concern A: \"This is just a negative result from a failed hypothesis\"\n53\tResponse: Frame as discovery paper. The equalizer effect is a positive finding—it was not the hypothesis we started with, but it's a genuine empirical phenomenon with theoretical implications. Many impactful papers come from unexpected findings.\n54\tRequired evidence: Strong standalone story; no mention of original entrainment hypothesis in the paper.\n55\t\n56\t### Concern B: \"Only one task shows the effect\" + Post-hoc taxonomy risk\n57\tResponse: Do NOT use binary retrieval/reasoning split — vulnerable to post-hoc cherry-picking (only 1 benchmark in \"retrieval\" category, exactly the one that's significant). Instead:\n58\t1. **Continuous spectrum**: Plot convergence strength (Spearman ρ) against a continuous measure of \"retrieval demand\" per benchmark. LAMBADA ρ=-1.0, ARC-E ρ=-0.7 (suggestive), ARC-C ρ=-0.3, HellaSwag/WinoGrande/MMLU near zero. Frame as dose-response: more retrieval demand → stronger convergence.\n59\t2. **Cite existing task taxonomies**: Use BIG-bench taxonomy or other published meta-analyses for task property classification — don't invent our own post-hoc.\n60\t3. **Honest reporting**: ARC-E ρ=-0.7 (p=0.117) is \"suggestive but underpowered\" — n=5 ratios means low statistical power. Continuous spectrum framing turns \"only LAMBADA significant\" into \"graded effect consistent with retrieval demand.\"\n61\t4. **Winogrande ambiguity**: Coreference resolution has a retrieval component (finding antecedent). Acknowledge this honestly rather than forcing it into \"reasoning.\"\n62\t5. **exp-002 validation**: If HellaSwag per-example analysis confirms no convergence at sample level, upgrades from \"aggregate not significant\" to \"per-example confirmed non-converging.\"\n63\tRequired experiments: exp-001 (done, PASS on dichotomy), exp-002 (per-example validation), exp-003 (sample characterization).\n64\t\n65\t### Concern C: \"No causal mechanism\"\n66\tResponse: Per-example analysis provides mechanistic evidence (different subsets). Strengthen with attention weight visualization and sample feature characterization.\n67\tRequired experiments: Sample characterization (exp-003), attention weight analysis (exp-004).\n68\t\n69\t### Concern D: \"n=3 architectures is too few\"\n70\tResponse: Three architectures with 5 attention ratios × 2 scales = 30 models. The per-example analysis provides thousands of data points. Check if additional architectures available.\n71\tRequired experiments: Architecture expansion check (exp-005).\n72\t\n73\t### Concern E: \"Two scales don't establish a trend\"\n74\tResponse: 7B unavailable (Wang et al. capped at 1.3B). Frame as \"binary contrast showing scale is a necessary condition\" — convergence requires sufficient model capacity (1.3B), not just attention. Strengthen via exp-007 (340M rebound mechanism analysis) to provide mechanistic explanation for the scale gap. Acknowledge as limitation; do not imply extrapolation.\n75\tRequired experiments: exp-007 (340M rebound mechanism).\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5fa48e70-09a4-461f-8d8b-adcdd4bc9307","uuid":"332eac70-d9ff-4336-a491-e1628fe9bc48","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:04:07.683Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T135121/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — entrainment_gated_ft\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n- Backup: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Deadline: ARR June 2026 cycle (submission ~June 15)\n\n## Paper Type\nEmpirical analysis paper (not method paper)\n\n## Core Claims (3 条)\n\n### Claim 1: Task-Specific Equalizer Effect\nAdding attention layers to different linear recurrent architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) induces aggregate performance convergence whose strength scales with task retrieval demand—strongest on LAMBADA (ρ=-1.0, p=0.017 two-tailed, cross-arch std drops 79%), suggestive on ARC-Easy (ρ=-0.7, p=0.233 two-tailed, underpowered at n=5), absent on reasoning-heavy tasks (HellaSwag, WinoGrande). This continuous relationship between retrieval demand and convergence strength is a key finding, not a limitation of single-task significance.\n\n### Claim 2: Scale-Dependent Convergence\nThe convergence effect is scale-dependent: monotonically complete at 1.3B parameters (Spearman ρ=-1.0 for cross-arch std vs attention ratio, exact permutation p=0.017, two-tailed) but only partial at 340M with a non-monotonic rebound at high attention ratios. Note: only two scale points available (Wang et al. do not provide 7B models); this is a binary contrast, not a multi-point trend. Paper must frame honestly as \"scale matters\" without implying extrapolation.\n\n### Claim 3: Architecture-Specific Effects (Equalizer Paradox)\nDespite aggregate convergence, attention acts on almost entirely different sample subsets per architecture (pairwise Jaccard < 0.10). GDN and RetNet are hurt (net negative on LAMBADA), HGRN2 is helped (net positive), with symmetric effect magnitudes—convergence emerges from complementary, not redundant, effects. Surface-level features (token frequency, context length) do NOT differentiate helped vs hurt samples (all p>0.2 except marginal RetNet context_length). This rules out trivial explanations. Deeper features (perplexity under baseline, topic/domain) are being tested. If those also fail, claim wording shifts to \"architecture-specific effect whose mechanism is opaque to standard features\" — the non-overlapping Jaccard evidence remains hard regardless.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n### Problem\nHybrid architectures mixing linear recurrence with full attention are becoming the dominant paradigm for efficient language models. A critical open question: does adding attention homogenize model behavior across different recurrence mechanisms, or do architecture-specific effects persist?\n\n### Existing Approaches & Gaps\nWang et al. (2025, arXiv 2507.06457) provide the most comprehensive model zoo (72 models, 3 architectures, multiple scales and attention ratios) but report only aggregate metrics. They qualitatively note convergence trends but do not analyze: (a) the per-example mechanism, (b) scale dependency of convergence, (c) task-specificity, or (d) why architectures converge to similar *aggregate* performance through *different* individual-sample effects.\n\n### Our Framing\nWe provide the first quantitative characterization of the \"equalizer effect\"—a systematic per-example analysis revealing that attention creates aggregate performance convergence through architecture-specific, largely non-overlapping mechanisms. This \"equalizer paradox\" (aggregate convergence + per-example divergence) has direct implications for architecture selection: even when aggregate metrics converge, the types of inputs each architecture handles well remain architecture-specific.\n\n### Headline Result Template\n\"At 1.3B scale, adding attention layers reduces cross-architecture performance variance by 79% on LAMBADA (std 0.024→0.005), yet per-example analysis reveals the affected sample subsets are almost entirely non-overlapping across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10), with symmetric but opposite effect directions.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n### Risk 1: Architecture Analysis vs NLP\nEMNLP is NLP-focused; this paper is closer to architecture analysis. **Mitigation**: Frame in terms of understanding language model behavior on specific linguistic tasks. Anchor analysis to token-level and context-level linguistic features (frequency, context length, retrieval demand). Include practical recommendations for practitioners choosing hybrid architectures for specific NLP tasks.\n\n### Risk 2: Limited Task Coverage\nCurrently only LAMBADA shows clear convergence. **Mitigation**: (a) Extend per-example analysis to HellaSwag and other benchmarks, (b) provide principled explanation of LAMBADA's retrieval-dependent nature vs reasoning tasks, (c) if convergence appears on other retrieval tasks, strengthens Claim 1; if not, reframe as \"convergence is task-type-specific\" which is still interesting.\n\n### Risk 3: Small Architecture Sample\nOnly 3 architectures (GDN, RetNet, HGRN2). **Mitigation**: These represent three distinct recurrence mechanisms (gated delta rule, linear attention with exponential decay, hierarchical gated recurrence). Check if Wang et al. have additional architectures or scales. n=3 is sufficient for the per-example analysis which is our main contribution.\n\n### Risk 4: Wang et al. Overlap\nThey have the model zoo AND qualitative convergence observation. **Mitigation**: Our contribution is explicitly about what they did NOT do: quantitative convergence framework, scale-dependent analysis, per-example mechanism analysis. Frame as \"using their excellent model zoo, we discover a deeper phenomenon they missed.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### Concern A: \"This is just a negative result from a failed hypothesis\"\nResponse: Frame as discovery paper. The equalizer effect is a positive finding—it was not the hypothesis we started with, but it's a genuine empirical phenomenon with theoretical implications. Many impactful papers come from unexpected findings.\nRequired evidence: Strong standalone story; no mention of original entrainment hypothesis in the paper.\n\n### Concern B: \"Only one task shows the effect\" + Post-hoc taxonomy risk\nResponse: Do NOT use binary retrieval/reasoning split — vulnerable to post-hoc cherry-picking (only 1 benchmark in \"retrieval\" category, exactly the one that's significant). Instead:\n1. **Continuous spectrum**: Plot convergence strength (Spearman ρ) against a continuous measure of \"retrieval demand\" per benchmark. LAMBADA ρ=-1.0, ARC-E ρ=-0.7 (suggestive), ARC-C ρ=-0.3, HellaSwag/WinoGrande/MMLU near zero. Frame as dose-response: more retrieval demand → stronger convergence.\n2. **Cite existing task taxonomies**: Use BIG-bench taxonomy or other published meta-analyses for task property classification — don't invent our own post-hoc.\n3. **Honest reporting**: ARC-E ρ=-0.7 (p=0.117) is \"suggestive but underpowered\" — n=5 ratios means low statistical power. Continuous spectrum framing turns \"only LAMBADA significant\" into \"graded effect consistent with retrieval demand.\"\n4. **Winogrande ambiguity**: Coreference resolution has a retrieval component (finding antecedent). Acknowledge this honestly rather than forcing it into \"reasoning.\"\n5. **exp-002 validation**: If HellaSwag per-example analysis confirms no convergence at sample level, upgrades from \"aggregate not significant\" to \"per-example confirmed non-converging.\"\nRequired experiments: exp-001 (done, PASS on dichotomy), exp-002 (per-example validation), exp-003 (sample characterization).\n\n### Concern C: \"No causal mechanism\"\nResponse: Per-example analysis provides mechanistic evidence (different subsets). Strengthen with attention weight visualization and sample feature characterization.\nRequired experiments: Sample characterization (exp-003), attention weight analysis (exp-004).\n\n### Concern D: \"n=3 architectures is too few\"\nResponse: Three architectures with 5 attention ratios × 2 scales = 30 models. The per-example analysis provides thousands of data points. Check if additional architectures available.\nRequired experiments: Architecture expansion check (exp-005).\n\n### Concern E: \"Two scales don't establish a trend\"\nResponse: 7B unavailable (Wang et al. capped at 1.3B). Frame as \"binary contrast showing scale is a necessary condition\" — convergence requires sufficient model capacity (1.3B), not just attention. Strengthen via exp-007 (340M rebound mechanism analysis) to provide mechanistic explanation for the scale gap. Acknowledge as limitation; do not imply extrapolation.\nRequired experiments: exp-007 (340M rebound mechanism).\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0184dDF1cCe6RsWmiDbmLPV1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of both the experiment data and paper framing. Let me also read the paper-writing-norms skill for style guidance since this is targeting EMNLP 2026.\n\nActually, looking at the instructions, I need to write the paper and compile it. Let me first check what's in the output directory and then load the paper-writing-norms skill.\n\nActually, the instructions say to just write the paper directly. Let me also check if the output directory exists.\n\nLet me now compose the full LaTeX paper based on:\n\n1. **Three core claims**: Task-specific equalizer effect, scale-dependent convergence, architecture-specific effects (equalizer paradox)\n\n\n2. **Seven experiments**: Multi-benchmark analysis, perplexity characterization, attention weight analysis, architecture expansion, statistical robustness, 340M rebound, convergence-divergence bridge\n3. **Narrative guidance**: Full equalizer paradox, three claims at original strength, dose-response across retrieval spectrum, perplexity mechanism, 4+ architectures\n\nI'll structure the paper with abstract, introduction, related work, method/setup, experiments mapped to sections and tables, discussion, and conclusion. Each of the seven experiments needs corresponding sections and tables in the paper.\n\nNow I'm mapping each experiment to its tables: multi-benchmark analysis goes to Table 2, the helped/hurt sample characterization to Tables 4-5, attention weights to Tables 6-7, architecture expansion to Tables 8-9, statistical robustness to Tables 3 and variants, the 340M rebound to Tables 10-11, and the convergence-divergence bridge to Table 12. I'll write this using a standard article class with proper formatting to keep it compilable.\n\nI'm structuring the paper with an abstract, introduction framing the equalizer paradox and three main claims, background and related work, experimental setup, then results sections covering dose-response convergence across benchmarks, statistical robustness, architecture expansion, the equalizer paradox itself, and mechanistic analysis.\n\nNow I need to finish laying out the remaining results sections on perplexity-based compensation, attention weight analysis, and scale-dependent convergence, followed by discussion, conclusion, and limitations. Before writing, I should verify the LaTeX setup and make sure I can compile the document without inventing any data.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13544,"cache_read_input_tokens":23467,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13544},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5fa48e70-09a4-461f-8d8b-adcdd4bc9307","uuid":"e488cf20-fb55-4d69-8f29-4995fe7a67cd"}
